    "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
    "problem": "If $\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},$ then $f(x)=$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$",
